# ZERO. Reached 2026-09-03. Read this before you raise it.
#
# History: 151 -> 91 -> 76 -> 62 -> 41 -> 23 -> 0.
#
# The last 62 were cleared without a single `as` cast. Four of them were not
# type noise at all, they were live defects the compiler had been pointing at
# the whole time:
#
#   * MatchupService.organizeRosterIntoLineup sorted the roster by
#     `b.points - a.points`, but HockeyPlayer has no top-level `points`
#     (season points live at `stats.points`). Every comparison was 0 - 0, so
#     the sort was a NO-OP and any team without a saved lineup got an
#     arbitrary starting lineup.
#   * DemoLeagueService had the same `hp.points || 0` mistake, always 0.
#   * extractScoringSettings passed a PARTIAL leagues.scoring_settings jsonb
#     straight into ScoringCalculator, which multiplies by an undefined
#     weight -> NaN points for a whole matchup. Now filled from
#     DEFAULT_SCORING per field.
#   * Roster.applyPlayerMove typed its target as `string` while one caller
#     passes a numeric HockeyPlayer.id, so `targetId.startsWith(...)` threw
#     TypeError and killed tap-to-swap.
#
# The bulk of the rest was one root cause: src/integrations/supabase/types.ts
# declared 21 tables when production has 180. Queries against undeclared
# tables resolved to `never`, which made the select-string parser recurse
# until it hit the instantiation limit (TS2589), and forced `as any` casts on
# the client elsewhere. Regenerated from the production project.
#
# WHY ZERO IS WORTH DEFENDING: the ratchet only fails when the count RISES.
# At 62 a new error was invisible. At 0 the very next one fails CI on the PR
# that introduced it, which is the only moment it is cheap to fix.
#
# If you are about to raise this number, you are proposing that a type error
# ships. Fix the error instead, or say plainly in the PR why it cannot be.
#
# NOTE: tsconfig.app.json still has strictNullChecks:false. Turning it on
# measured 134 errors on 2026-09-03 and is a separate, planned migration
# (tsconfig "Phase 2"). Until then TypeScript will NOT narrow a union through
# `!x.ok` or an else-branch; `x.ok === false` does narrow. Several fixes in
# this repo depend on that distinction and say so at the site.
